Staphylococcal Food Poisoning
Hydration, Fluid and Volume abnormalities Gastrointestinal Loss
Explosive vomiting 1-6 hours post-ingestion, Severe crampy abdominal pain, Absence of significant fever

VISIT CONTEXT

Case ID: HFV-045
Reason for Visit: Sudden, explosive onset of severe vomiting and stomach cramps shortly after eating, resulting in profound weakness and inability to stand, requiring urgent evaluation for severe acute dehydration.
Location: Private residence
Present at Visit: Patient and spouse
Urgency Justification: Telephone triage noted a sudden, violent onset of uncontrollable vomiting and severe abdominal pain occurring just two hours after a meal, leading to near-syncope upon standing. This required an urgent after-hours assessment to definitively rule out acute surgical emergencies, confirm hemodynamic stability, and provide parenteral antiemetics to safely initiate oral rehydration prior to the next morning's in-hours period.

SUBJECTIVE

Chief Complaint: Uncontrollable, violent vomiting, severe stomach cramps, and feeling extremely faint. Patient states, I ate lunch, and two hours later my stomach cramped violently, and I have not stopped vomiting since; I feel completely drained.
History of Present Illness: The patient reports an explosive, sudden onset of intractable nausea and severe vomiting. The presentation is characterized by an extremely short incubation period, occurring exactly 3 hours after consuming a catered sandwich lunch (suspected contaminated food). This was immediately followed by severe, crampy abdominal pain. In the last hour, watery, non-bloody diarrhea has developed following the onset of vomiting. Notably, the patient reports an absence of significant fever or chills. Due to the rapid, massive fluid loss, the patient experiences severe orthostatic dizziness and near-syncope when attempting to ambulate. Extreme lethargy and generalized weakness are profound.
Functional Status: Bedbound due to profound weakness and ongoing emesis; fully reliant on spouse.
Social/Safety Concerns: High risk of hypovolemic collapse if fluid losses are not halted; spouse is highly supportive.

OBJECTIVE

Vitals: BP: 105/70 mmHg (Supine) -> 85/55 mmHg (Standing) | HR: 112 bpm | Temp: 36.8 C | RR: 18 bpm | SpO2: 99% | Wt: 78.0 Kg
General: Patient is pale, actively retching into a basin, diaphoretic, and appears acutely exhausted.
Physical Exam: Cardiovascular: Tachycardia is marked, completely out of proportion to the normal temperature, reflecting significant volume depletion. A profound orthostatic drop in blood pressure is objectively confirmed. Respiratory: Lungs are clear. Gastrointestinal: The diagnostic triad is clinically classic. The history confirms explosive vomiting 1-6 hours post-ingestion. The patient is experiencing severe crampy abdominal pain, with diffuse tenderness on palpation but no focal rigidity or rebound to suggest peritonitis. Bowel sounds are hyperactive. There is an absence of significant fever. Integumentary: Examination reveals decreased skin turgor and extremely dry oral mucous membranes, confirming acute, severe dehydration.
Environmental Observations: Clean environment. Multiple emesis basins utilized.
Medication Reconciliation: Checked against EHR. Patient takes no daily medications.

ASSESSMENT

Primary Diagnosis: Staphylococcal Food Poisoning - Status: Acute, decompensated volume status
Differentials:

Bacterial Enteritis (e.g., Salmonella, Campylobacter) - Ruled out/unlikely due to: The extremely rapid incubation period (hours rather than days), the predominance of explosive upper gastrointestinal symptoms (vomiting) over isolated bloody diarrhea, and the strict absence of fever.

Acute Appendicitis - Ruled out/unlikely due to: The hyperacute, explosive onset of massive vomiting preceding the pain, the diffuse crampy nature of the pain without right lower quadrant localization, and the lack of systemic fever.

Viral Gastroenteritis (Norovirus) - Ruled out/unlikely due to: The extremely short 3-hour incubation period linked specifically to a single meal, whereas norovirus typically presents 12-48 hours post-exposure.
Risk Level: Environmental/Social Risk: Low

CLINICAL REASONING SUMMARY

The patient's presentation of hyperacute, explosive vomiting and crampy abdominal pain occurring mere hours after food ingestion, strictly in the absence of fever, is pathognomonic for Staphylococcal Food Poisoning (preformed enterotoxin ingestion). The sheer volume of sudden emesis has caused rapid, massive intravascular volume depletion, evidenced by significant tachycardia, dry mucous membranes, and profound orthostatic hypotension. Delaying assessment until the next in-hours period would have left the patient at severe risk of a syncopal fall or progressive hypovolemia requiring emergency intravenous resuscitation. Urgent after-hours assessment was required to definitively rule out a surgical abdomen, confirm the benign nature of the self-limiting toxin exposure, and administer an immediate parenteral antiemetic to halt the vomiting cycle, thereby allowing safe, structured oral rehydration at home. The expected investigation results include an absence of fecal leukocytes or erythrocytes on stool microscopy, with resolution of symptoms before standard bacterial culture yields results that would assist in confirming the diagnosis, rendering immediate testing unnecessary.

CLINICIAN CARE PLAN

Clinical Interventions: Administered Ondansetron 4mg sublingually to rapidly control nausea and vomiting. Instructed the spouse to initiate strict oral rehydration therapy using an ice-cold commercial electrolyte solution only after 30 minutes of no further emesis.
Positive Behavioral Change: To rebuild your fluid levels without triggering another violent vomiting episode, commit to taking one single teaspoon (5ml) of an ice-cold electrolyte solution every 10 minutes. Use a timer. When the timer goes off, take the sip, and verbally acknowledge that the fluid stayed down. The steady reduction in your intense dizziness will reward your patience and strict compliance with this micro-dosing process.
Home-Based Services (Optional): None.
DME/Safety (Optional): None.
Follow-up: Routine GP follow-up only if symptoms fail to resolve entirely within 24 hours.

SAFETY-NETTING ESCALATION PLAN

Proceed to the nearest Emergency Department or call 000 if you cannot keep the teaspoon of fluid down for the next 4 hours despite the anti-nausea medication, if you develop a high fever, or if your abdominal pain becomes constant, rigid, and severe.

PATIENT INFORMATION

Disclaimer: This diagnosis assumes a self-limiting intoxication based on classic symptom timing; severe worsening requires immediate reassessment.
Clinical Course: You ingested a pre-formed bacterial toxin in your food, causing your stomach to violently reject it. The intense phase typically burns out rapidly within 12 to 24 hours. The critical intervention is slowly replacing the massive amount of fluid you lost to restore your blood pressure.

SAFETY-NETTING ADVICE

Do not attempt to drink large glasses of water or sports drinks. Rapidly filling your irritated stomach will immediately trigger further violent vomiting, dangerously worsening your dehydration.

PATIENT EDUCATION

